### New defects introduced by these edits

**NEW-1 (MINOR) — phase-02 scan-state keying contradicts the claimed cross-refresh progress.** The model buffers rows "keyed by the opaque scan-token ID" with "token lost/invalid → restart the scan from `fetchTo`" (phase-02:21–30), but the service creates `token := ""` per `ensure()` call — the token is request-scoped and discarded at request end (service.go:301). So under the literal model, every refresh regenerates a new token → the previous scan's buffered rows are unreachable → restart-per-request, not progress. Yet the updated Known-limitation says "refreshes advance the scan `BLOCKSCOUT_MAX_SCAN_PAGES` pages at a time via the progress token" (phase-02:97–98) — cumulative cross-refresh progress the token-keyed model cannot deliver. (The previous wording "refreshes re-scan and burn quota" was actually *more* accurate.) Resumable progress requires the buffer keyed deterministically by `(net, wallet, fetchFrom)` so a fresh request re-derives it — that also handles `fetchTo` drift naturally since the new scan descends through the head segment first. Fix: either pin deterministic wallet+window-keyed resumption, or correct the limitation sentence to say refreshes restart and re-burn the same newest pages (and consider WindowCap as the practical bound). As written, two sections of the same file assert incompatible mechanisms — an implementer cannot satisfy both.

**NEW-2 (NIT) — buffer overflow mapped to `degraded`** (phase-02:23). A local resource cap is not "upstream answered but signalled degraded," and under phase-12 `degraded` is a failover kind → a deterministic local overflow burns the fallback chain on a window that hits the identical cap. `internal` (or an explicit note that the failover-on-degraded consequence is intended) is more truthful and cheaper.

Everything else from R4 is genuinely closed — D5's enumeration is thorough, and the never-list is now closed across all ErrKinds.

